WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.455
Cross-site scripting is browser-side injection.

2
00:00:03.455 --> 00:00:10.848
It happens when untrusted content is interpreted as executable script or unsafe markup in a browser context.

3
00:00:10.848 --> 00:00:24.268
JavaScript developers need to think about server-rendered HTML, DOM insertion points, frontend framework behavior, HTML attributes, URLs, CSS, JavaScript contexts, and JSON embedded in pages.

4
00:00:24.268 --> 00:00:27.964
The browser is an interpreter with many sub-contexts.

5
00:00:27.964 --> 00:00:31.500
Output encoding depends on where the data is placed.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.241
Text inside an HTML element is not the same as data inside an attribute.

7
00:00:36.241 --> 00:00:40.339
A URL context is different from a JavaScript string context.

8
00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.589
CSS and JSON have their own rules.

9
00:00:42.589 --> 00:00:47.812
One generic escaping function cannot safely handle every browser location.

10
00:00:47.812 --> 00:00:54.562
Developers should use framework-supported safe rendering patterns and context-aware encoding.

11
00:00:54.562 --> 00:00:56.651
Safe DOM APIs help reduce risk.

12
00:00:56.651 --> 00:01:01.714
Setting text content is different from asking the browser to parse markup.

13
00:01:01.714 --> 00:01:07.741
Building elements through APIs is generally safer than injecting large strings of HTML.

14
00:01:07.741 --> 00:01:17.223
When HTML sanitization is necessary, it should be done with a well-reviewed library and a clear understanding of what the sanitizer allows.

15
00:01:17.223 --> 00:01:20.598
Sanitization is not a substitute for good design.

16
00:01:20.598 --> 00:01:26.223
Frontend frameworks often provide escaping by default, but escape hatches exist.

17
00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:34.580
Features that render raw markup, build dynamic URLs, bind styles, or integrate third-party widgets deserve extra review.

18
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:43.901
Server-provided data should still be treated as untrusted from the browser perspective unless the context and encoding are controlled.

19
00:01:43.901 --> 00:01:52.258
Content Security Policy can provide defense in depth by limiting certain resource loading and script execution patterns.

20
00:01:52.258 --> 00:01:57.481
It does not replace output encoding, safe DOM APIs, or server-side controls.

21
00:01:57.481 --> 00:02:01.419
Client-side validation also does not protect the server.

22
00:02:01.419 --> 00:02:11.785
Strong browser-side injection prevention combines safe rendering, context-aware encoding, review of escape hatches, and layered browser defenses.
